To overcome these issues, we developed a new hybrid biomimetic motor system consisting of a MOF and diphenylalanine (DPA) peptide. MOFs, consisting of metal ions and bridging organic ligands, have recently emerged as an important family of nanoscale porous materials because of their unique structural and functional properties<sup>10–18</sup>. In this work, a MOF is selected for the peptide storage because of its capability to assemble small molecules in the highly ordered pore array of the coordination framework and release guest molecules in a more isotropic direction via bond-breaking of the framework than in existing porous systems<sup>19</sup>. MOFs are also advantageous as vehicles of autonomous motors because their rigidity, density, crystalline pore organization and pore size can be designed such that the ideal combination of metal ions and ligands of MOFs can be selected for optimal light weight for the swimming motion and to have a tailored structure to release and reorganize guest peptides for their controlled motion at air/liquid interfaces. The self-assembling nature of peptides<sup>20–27</sup> is also appropriate as a guest molecule to power the MOF motor because the released peptides being re-assembled at the MOF/water interface in a highly ordered structure are expected to generate a large surface tension gradient, favouring a strong Marangoni effect<sup>5</sup> and stronger propulsion towards the higher surface tension side of the MOF. This novel driving mechanism of peptide-incorporating MOF on a water surface consists of two steps. The first step is the release of DPA peptides from the MOF by mixing sodium ethylenediaminetetraacetate (Na-EDTA) with the system. The partial destruction of the MOF by EDTA allows the slow release of DPAs through the ordered pores of the MOF. The second step is the accumulation and self-assembly of DPAs at the water/MOF interface to create a large surface tension difference around the MOF, thus providing the swimming motion, as the released DPA assembly on the MOF creates a hydrophobic domain. The MOF motor moves in the direction of the higher surface tension side. This peptide–MOF system has advantages over other artificial motor microsystems in accomplishing the development of more efficient artificial motor systems with faster
movement and longer mobility lifetime because the MOF can load peptide fuels at a much higher density in its highly ordered porous framework\(^{28,29}\). Another advantage of using a porous MOF for peptide storage is its highly organized pore geometry, where the released DPA peptides can be robustly self-assembled at the MOF/water interface as the well-aligned guest peptides are released slowly from the MOF. In nature, biological motors convert chemical free energy to mechanical power directly by creating isothermal and non-equilibrium conditions through biochemical reactions\(^{3,5,30}\). To avoid equilibrium, cells modify their environment by metabolizing resources and producing products and waste, while simultaneously the system exploits the new resources while preventing the inhibitory effects of waste products\(^{1}\). The DPA-peptide-incorporating MOF motor also creates a non-equilibrium condition by releasing peptides and converting this chemical change into the energy of motor, generated by the reconfiguration resulting from DPA peptide self-assembly, thus resembling the metabolization and production of resources in nature. This type of technology could be applied to develop miniaturized robotic systems that could sense target chemicals, move toward their location, and then store them inside the porous frameworks. The outcome of this work may also shed light on the fundamental mechanisms of biological motors and chemotaxis in natural systems.
