When the DPA peptides released onto the water interface are collected on transmission electron microscopy (TEM) grids, the TEM images and electron diffraction patterns show that the DPA peptides are assembled into a single crystalline structure (Fig. 3a; ref. 39). Because salts and ligand residues from MOF fragments are washed out before imaging, these crystals are assembled from DPA nanocrystals. This result is consistent with a swimming mechanism where the generation of DPA crystals at the interface creates a surface tension gradient, effectively producing motor activity. Here it is interesting that the resulting DPA crystals are of a planar parallelogram shape rather than the typical tubular form21,40,41. The difference in the crystallization environment on different assembling substrates leads to a polymorphic nature of peptide crystal forms via different nucleation pathways42. Because nucleation of DPA peptides occurs on the 2D air–liquid surface after release from the MOF, the DPA crystals tend to be self-assembled in the planar form rather than the cylindrical form. Moreover,
As the loaded DPA peptides are considered as fuels, MOFs with more loaded DPA should run for a longer timeframe. To confirm this expected behaviour, we examined the DPA-load dependence of the duration of motor motion with different loading amounts of DPA: 10, 20 and \(30\%\) weight ratios to the MOF. When DPA-loaded MOF particles of a diameter \(1\,\mathrm{mm}\) are dropped on the aqueous solution containing EDTA, the durations of MOF movement for 10, 20 and \(30\%\) DPA-loading are approximately 300, 600 and \(900\,\mathrm{s}\), respectively, as shown in Fig. 3c. This observation is consistent with our hypothesis that the duration of MOF motion becomes longer as the loaded amount of DPA peptide fuel is increased. Interestingly, the velocity of MOF motion does not correlate with the load. This result indicates that the velocity is not dependent on the amount of loaded DPAs, and thus the degree and the rate of spatial inhomogeneity are more important driving forces for the MOF motor motion.
In conclusion, hybrid biomolecular MOF assemblies incorporating the DPA peptides into a highly ordered porous MOF structure behave as autonomous biochemical motors as the DPA–MOF particles are dropped onto a water solution containing EDTA. After the MOF is partially decomposed at the interface, DPA is slowly released from inside the MOF, creating a surface tension gradient that triggers autonomous motion. The freshly reorganized peptide crystalline domain is continuously generated at the interface as the slow release of DPA continues, enabling long-lasting motion with a slow saturation of DPA on the interface. Eventually the surface tension gradient drops as the size of the DPA domain dominates the hydrophilic area with an increased number of DPA crystals on the interface, and the motion of the MOF slows and
eventually stops after consuming all the DPA inside the MOF. It should be emphasized that the energy source of this generator is simply the free energy change of peptide emission and bears a similarity to biological motors, which also work through the dissipation of chemical energy. There are few artificial motor systems that have the means to store energy and release it by emitting hydrophobic peptides in a highly re-assembled structure to trigger mechanical motion via a surface tension gradient. Although primarily this work demonstrates the creation of a novel biomimetic artificial motor, our study of this system may also provide a better understanding of the mechanism of energy transduction and hemotaxis in biological systems. The direction and the motion of MOF particles could be controlled by creating a pH gradient or an EDTA concentration gradient on liquid interfaces43. This type of artificial peptide-motor system could be developed into the building blocks for targeted drug injection, osmotic pumping devices and micro-actuators. It could be further developed into smart machines with features that can change characteristically, by sensing and adapting to new environments3.
## Methods
Synthesis of MOFs and incorporation of DPA in the MOFs. The MOFs used here were prepared according to previously reported methods31. In the case of \(20\%\) DPA incorporation in the MOF, after \(468\,\mathrm{mg}\) of DPA was dissolved in \(1\,\mathrm{ml}\) of 1,1,1,3,3,3-hexafluoro-2-propanol, MOF, \(2.34\,\mathrm{g}\), was added and the solvent extracted by rotary vacuum. The resulting solid DPA–MOF compound was further dried under vacuum for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\). For the 10 and \(30\%\) DPA incorporation, 234 and \(702\,\mathrm{mg}\) of DPA were dissolved in the same volume of 1,1,1,3,3,3-hexafluoro-2-propanol, respectively, and then mixed with \(2.34\,\mathrm{g}\) of MOF. The solvent was extracted in the same manner as described above.
Analysis of motion of hybrid DPA–MOF. For each experiment, a granular conglomerate of DPA-loaded MOF, neat DPA, or neat MOF of approximately \(1\,\mathrm{mm}\)
diameter was added an aqueous solution in \(16\,\mathrm{ml}\) (or \(200\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\)) of \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) EDTA in NaOH \((2\,\mathrm{mM})\) in a transparent plastic Petri dish (50 mm diameter and 7 mm depth or \(8\,\mathrm{mm}\) diameter and \(4\,\mathrm{mm}\) depth). The lateral motion on the liquid surface was monitored using halogen lamp illumination from below through the light diffusing plate of a light microscope. The resulting movies were recorded as top-view video files at a frame rate of 24.39 frames per second. These sequential images are binarized to extract the particle motions, whose velocity change was analysed by the image processing software, ImageJ, and its plugin, Mtrack2 (http://valelab.ucsf.edu/\~nico/IJplugins/MTrack2.html).
Instrumentation. For TEM, samples on the aqueous solution were directly transferred to a carbon-coated copper grid by dipping them at the interface. After the grid was washed in pure water, with resistivity \(18.2\,\mathrm{M}\Omega\,\mathrm{cm}\), these samples were dried at room temperature. The samples were characterized by TEM (JEOL-JEM 2100) with an acceleration voltage of \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\). X-ray powder diffraction data were collected on a Rigaku RINT 2000 Ultima diffractometer with \(\mathrm{Cu}\,\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation. The adsorption isotherms of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(17\,\mathrm{K}\) were measured with a BELSORF-1mm volumetric adsorption instrument using \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas of high purity \((99.99999\%)\). Particle size distributions were measured by a HORIBA Partica LA-950 laser diffraction particle size analyser. Brewster angle micrographs were taken with an Accurion I-Elli2000 Imaging Ellipsometer.
